An agent that can only read is safe and not very useful. The moment it can refund a customer, merge a pull request, delete a table or wire money, someone has to decide which actions run on their own and which wait for a person. Writing "please ask me before touching prod" in CLAUDE.md is a request, not a control. A real approval gate sits between the agent and the tool, holds the call, asks the right human, and only releases it on a recorded yes.

This post compares ten ways to build that gate in 2026: five new hosted or self-hosted approval products (withHuman, Pushary, HumanAuth, VeriProof, Crios), one team workspace (HumanLayer), one authorization vendor's MCP gateway (Permit.io), and three built-in mechanisms in frameworks you may already use (LangGraph interrupts, OpenAI Agents SDK approvals, Claude Code permission hooks). Short version: withHuman is the best purpose-built open source option, Pushary is the best for approving coding agents from your phone, HumanAuth and VeriProof are the picks when an auditor will ask who approved what, and the three framework features are the free baseline if you are happy to build the reviewer side yourself. Prices, limits and star counts are as of November 27, 2026.

{% youtube id="IkaPHiMDazM" title="Hooks in Claude Code (official Claude channel)" /%}

Official walkthrough of Claude Code hooks from Anthropic's Claude channel, the mechanism behind the "Claude Code permission hooks" entry below. I confirmed the title and channel with YouTube's oEmbed endpoint; I did not watch it end to end.

## Short answer: quick picks

- **Best overall for gating tool calls:** withHuman. Open source core (Apache-2.0), rule pipelines, many reviewer channels, and a free hosted tier for up to 5 members.
- **Best free option:** the framework features (LangGraph `interrupt()`, OpenAI Agents SDK `needs_approval`, Claude Code hooks) cost nothing beyond what you already pay. Among products, withHuman Free ($0, up to 5 members) and HumanAuth (1,000 requests a month free) have real free tiers.
- **Best open source:** withHuman (Apache-2.0) for a product; LangGraph (MIT) and the OpenAI Agents SDK (MIT) for libraries.
- **Best for coding agents on your phone:** Pushary ($9.99 a month, lock-screen approve/deny, optional phone call).
- **Best for audit evidence:** HumanAuth (signed, offline-verifiable receipts) and VeriProof (tamper-evident decision records, from $995 a month billed annually).
- **Best for MCP tool governance in an enterprise:** Permit.io's MCP Gateway (human-in-the-loop is an Enterprise feature).
- **Best self-hosted agent platform with approvals built in:** Crios (pricing is by contact; I could not find public prices).
- **Best for teams steering coding agents together:** HumanLayer, with the caveat that it is a collaboration workspace more than an approval gate.

{% callout type="warning" %}
Five of these products are young. withHuman's own launch post on X is dated October 15, 2026, and the Pushary founder's LinkedIn lists the company from December 2025 (Pushary's About page says the operating company was founded in 2024). I confirmed that each official site loads and describes human approval for agents, and I read their pricing and docs pages. I did not install or run any of them, so treat the capability lines as vendor-described, not tested.
{% /callout %}

