## What a human-in-the-loop gate actually has to do

Before the product sections, it helps to agree on what "approval" means, because the tools below solve different halves of it.

1. **Intercept.** The call must be held before it reaches the tool. A prompt instruction does not do this. A hook, a gateway, a framework pause or an application check does.
2. **Decide who decides.** A rule picks auto-approve, deny or ask, and, if it asks, which person, team or on-call rotation.
3. **Reach the human.** Slack, email, a phone push, a phone call, a dashboard. An approval that nobody sees is a stalled agent.
4. **Fail safe.** If nobody answers, the default must be deny (or keep waiting), never an accidental yes.
5. **Resume.** The agent continues from where it paused, with the decision, and ideally with the arguments the human actually saw.
6. **Record.** Who said yes, to what exact action, when. Some tools go further and make the record verifiable by a third party.

Most products here are strong on three or four of these. The framework features (LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Code hooks) are strong on 1 and 5 and leave 2, 3, 4 and 6 to you.

## 1. withHuman: the open source approval layer

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/withhuman.png" name="withHuman" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** withHuman describes itself as "the approval layer for AI agents": it intercepts a consequential tool call, runs it through an approval pipeline you define, and, when the rules say so, sends it to a human reviewer. Its homepage walks through a support agent that tries a $75 `stripe.refund`; a rule auto-approves refunds under $20, anything more goes to the Payments team's on-call person, who approves in Slack, Discord, email, SMS, WhatsApp or the iPhone and Android apps. The founders' launch post on X is dated October 15, 2026, so this is a very new product.

{% x id="2110745909139300398" /%}

**Who it is for.** Teams that want a framework-agnostic gate for both coding agents and their own production agents, and that value an open spec. withHuman is built on AAP, the Agent Approval Protocol, which it says it started as an open spec so that "your integrations are with AAP, not withHuman." The spec, SDKs, adapters and conformance tests are described as open source, and the core (queue, audit trail, web UI) is self-hostable. The GitHub repo `withHumanAI/withHuman` is Apache-2.0 licensed and had 23 stars as of November 27, 2026, last pushed November 25, 2026. Treat that star count as a sign of how early it is.

**Key features.**
- Approval pipelines with rules such as "automatic below $20, ask a human above."
- Escalation paths and team escalation policies, so a request goes to whoever is on call.
- Hooks for coding agents. The site says the CLI connects Claude Code, Codex, OpenClaw, Pi, Hermes and DeepSeek on the machine.
- An MCP gateway and an MCP server, plus an HTTP API (`POST /api/aap/v1/requests`) and AAP SDKs for LangGraph, CrewAI, Mastra and others.
- Reviewer identity on every decision, an append-only history, and the full request kept with each decision.

```bash
curl -fsSL https://downloads.withhuman.ai/install.sh | sh -s -- onboard
```

```json
// HTTP API body shown on the site for POST /api/aap/v1/requests
{
  "tool": "issue_refund",
  "server": "stripe",
  "arguments": { "amount": 4900, "reason": "duplicate_charge" },
  "timeout": "30m"
}
```

**Pricing (withhuman.ai/pricing, as of November 27, 2026).**

| Plan | Price | Members | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Open Source | Free | Self-hosted | Hooks, AAP API, MCP server, approval pipelines and webhook blocks included; MCP gateway, gateway tunnels and AI judge not included |
| Free | $0 hosted | Up to 5 | Unlimited agents and approval requests; MCP gateway included; AI judge "Limited" |
| Scale | $20 per user per month | Unlimited | Everything in Free, AI judge included, pipeline suggestions marked "coming soon" |
| Enterprise | Custom annual contract | Unlimited | Cloud, on-prem or BYOC; SSO, SCIM, SIEM export, dedicated support |

**Pros**
- Real free tier with unlimited agents and approval requests, and a self-hostable Apache-2.0 core.
- Open protocol reduces lock-in; you can in theory swap providers.
- Rule pipelines mean small actions never interrupt a human.
- Widest reviewer-channel list in this roundup (Slack, Discord, email, text, WhatsApp, mobile apps).

**Cons**
- Very new, 23 GitHub stars, little public production track record.
- The open source edition lacks the MCP gateway and AI judge; those are hosted features.
- Some Scale features (pipeline suggestions) and an organization metrics view are labelled "coming soon" on the pricing page.

**Benefits for your use case.** You can start with the free hosted tier, put one risky tool (refunds, deploys, `git push`) behind a rule, and keep the option to self-host later. The "who approved what" record answers the first question a manager or auditor asks.

**Standout use case.** Customer-support or finance agents where small actions should run automatically and large ones need a named human.

**Skip it if** you need cryptographic proof that a specific human approved (look at HumanAuth), or you need a vendor with a multi-year track record.

{% figure src="https://pushary.com/og-ai-coding.webp?v=2" alt="Pushary official social image showing AI coding agent approvals" caption="Pushary's official site image for its coding-agent approvals. Source: pushary.com" /%}

## 2. Pushary: approvals on your phone, with a phone call if you miss it

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/pushary.png" name="Pushary" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Pushary is "human-in-the-loop for AI agents": when an agent needs a yes, the question lands on your phone or Mac, you approve or deny, and the agent continues. For your own coding agents it installs permission hooks and a policy engine (auto-approve, deny, escalate and timeout rules per tool). For companies building agents, a Partner API lets the product's own end users approve. It is built by Aadil Ghani and operated by RalphNex OÜ, an Estonian company (registry code 16932562, per its own site). Its Product Hunt page lists the "Pushary Calls" launch of June 2, 2026.

{% x id="2124542827279319267" /%}

**Who it is for.** Solo developers and small teams running Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Hermes and similar agents who get stuck waiting at a permission prompt while away from the keyboard. The founder's own posts describe an agent that waited 47 minutes for one yes.

**Key features.**
- iPhone lock-screen approve or deny; on Android, Deny is one tap and Approve opens the app (per the site).
- A phone call if the push is missed, after 1, 2 or 3 minutes of your choosing, in calling hours you set, to numbers in the US, Canada, Germany and several European countries (per the site).
- Per-tool policies: auto-approve safe commands, deny destructive ones, escalate risky ones; a timer rule decides what silence means.
- A cross-agent audit trail, append-only and exportable on Team, with the answerer's name when the answer comes from the Pushary apps.
- One-command setup: `npx -y pushary@latest setup`, which the site says wires the MCP server, permission hooks and event tracking. Adapters are listed for the Vercel AI SDK (`@pushary/ai-sdk`), LangGraph, CrewAI, Mastra, the OpenAI Agents SDK and others. I confirmed the `pushary` package exists on npm and PyPI and that `@pushary/ai-sdk` and `@pushary/server` exist on npm.

```bash
npx -y pushary@latest setup
```

**Pricing (pushary.com/pricing, as of November 27, 2026).** Monthly prices shown; a yearly toggle advertises "2 months free."

| Plan | Price | Highlights |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Agent | $9.99 per month | One person, every agent; 5K notifications a month; permission policies; Mac app; 30-day history; 10 call minutes a month |
| Agent Pro | $19.99 per month | Unlimited notifications; budgets; Policy Autopilot suggestions; 90-day history; 20 call minutes |
| Team | $59 per month for 5 people | Shared audit trail with export; team activity; 365-day history; 60 call minutes. The homepage lists $12 a month per extra person |
| Partner Launch | $99 per month | Up to 5K of your users, 25K approval requests a month, 3 operator seats |
| Partner Growth | $299 per month | Up to 25K users, 100K requests a month, 10 seats |
| Partner Scale | $799 per month | Up to 100K users, 500K requests a month, 25 seats |
| Enterprise | Custom | Contact sales |

A workspace's first paid plan starts with a 3-day free trial; there is no permanent free plan. The Pushary pricing page itself says plainly that Claude Code Remote Control "is good" and is enough if Claude Code is the only agent you run.

**Pros**
- The best "away from my desk" experience here: push, then a phone call.
- Works across many coding agents with one rule set and one record.
- Honest about limits: its FAQ says an MCP connection alone does not intercept every tool call, and a yes typed at the agent's own prompt is not recorded.
- Public "receipts" page: the founder's own workspace showed 214 decisions, 40.2% auto-cleared by policy and a 3 second median approval latency over a 7 day window ending July 6, 2026 (vendor-reported, one user).

**Cons**
- No free tier beyond a 3-day trial, and the core product is closed source (only a small MIT skill repo, `pushary/pushary-skill`, 1 star as of today).
- Enforcement depends on supported hooks or on your application gating the action.
- The server-side policy engine for products you build is described on its About page as "being built."
- Calls ring only certain countries, and some agents (Cursor) are not covered by calls, per the founder's Product Hunt reply.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your pain is "the agent stopped and I did not notice," Pushary attacks that directly. For least-privilege setups, per-tool deny rules plus a human gate on the rest give you a workable policy for cloud coding agents without building a reviewer app.

**Standout use case.** Long-running Claude Code or Codex sessions on a remote box while you are away.

{% youtube id="rEcI1MYLdjc" title="Approve Claude Code and Codex From Your Mac's Notch or Phone (RalphNex, Pushary's operator)" /%}

Vendor demo from RalphNex, the company that operates Pushary. Title and channel confirmed through YouTube oEmbed; I did not watch it end to end.

{% x id="2124848540799438935" /%}

**Skip it if** you only use Claude Code with a paid Claude plan (Remote Control may be enough), or you need a self-hosted open source gate.

## 3. HumanAuth: approvals with signed, offline-verifiable receipts


**What it is.** HumanAuth is "human-in-the-loop approval for AI agents, with signed proof." Your agent calls `authorize()` for a risky action (a wire, a payout, a merge); the platform pushes a plain-language request to the approver's phone or Mac; the approver confirms with Face ID or Touch ID; the device signs with a key that exists only on that device; the platform countersigns; and the service that executes the action verifies the receipt offline. Its argument against "pause-and-ping" tools is that the approval becomes a boolean in a database that nothing downstream can check.

**Who it is for.** Teams in finance, healthcare, legal, insurance or any workflow where "who approved this" must survive an audit, and where the executing service can be changed to demand a receipt.

**Key features.**
- Dual-signed receipt bound to a plan hash (the parameters the human saw), single-use and replay-safe, verified offline with EdDSA and JWKS.
- Quorum approvals ("2 of 3"), approver groups, TTLs from 10 seconds to 1 hour (24 hours on Enterprise), and webhooks.
- Three MCP tools: `human_authorize`, `human_collect`, `human_inform`; a TypeScript SDK, a verifier library, a CLI and an OpenAPI 3.1 REST API.
- Integrations listed for LangChain, CrewAI, AutoGen, the OpenAI Agents SDK and n8n.
- The receipt spec, verifier, SDK, CLI and MCP server are described as MIT licensed and published on npm (I confirmed `@humanauth/cli`, `@humanauth/sdk` and `@humanauth/mcp` exist on npm). The platform itself can be self-deployed into your own Cloudflare account under an Enterprise agreement.

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "humanauth": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@humanauth/mcp"],
      "env": { "HUMANAUTH_API_KEY": "ha_live_..." }
    }
  }
}
```

The snippet above is the vendor's published MCP config with the key left as a placeholder; I did not run it.

**Pricing (humanauth.ai/pricing, as of November 27, 2026).**

| Plan | Price | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Free allowance | 1,000 approval requests a month free | No card required; 200 requests a day fair-use guard |
| Pay as you go | $0.03 per request after 1,000; $0.02 past 100k a month; $0.01 past 1M | Humans, devices and tenants are never billed. The FAQ says to email for early access when you hit the allowance, so it does not read as fully self-serve |
| Enterprise | Contact | 99.9% SLA, TTLs up to 24 hours, SSO and SCIM, multi-year evidence archive, legal hold and evidence export, committed-use discounts |

**Pros**
- The strongest evidence story here: a receipt that a third party can verify without trusting HumanAuth's uptime.
- Usage-based pricing with unlimited humans, and a real free allowance.
- Biometric approval on the approver's device addresses "rubber stamp from a phished account."

**Cons**
- You must change the executing service to demand and verify a receipt, which is more work than a pause-and-resume hook.
- Moving beyond the free 1,000 requests appears to need a manual step (email).
- Young product with no independent reviews that I could find.

**Benefits for your use case.** For high-value actions (money movement, data release, production deploys) the receipt turns "an agent said a human agreed" into something checkable.

**Skip it if** you only need a quick Slack-style approval for low-risk actions, where the receipt machinery is overkill.

{% figure src="https://humanauth.ai/og.png" alt="HumanAuth official social card" caption="HumanAuth's official site card. Source: humanauth.ai" /%}

## 4. VeriProof: governance, review routing and sealed decision records

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/veriproof.png" name="VeriProof" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** VeriProof (veriproof.app) is an AI agent governance product: your application sends a proposed action, VeriProof checks it against business rules, returns allow, deny or "needs review," routes exceptions to an authorized reviewer, and seals the rule result, approval and reported outcome in a tamper-evident record. Its demo follows a claims agent with a $15,000 authority limit: a $12,800 claim proceeds, an over-limit payment is held and sent to a reviewer in Slack, Microsoft Teams, ServiceNow or email.

**Who it is for.** Regulated or risk-averse enterprises moving agents from pilot to production that need to show auditors the control. The site maps decisions to frameworks including the EU AI Act, NIST AI RMF 1.0, ISO/IEC 42001:2023, HIPAA, GDPR Article 22 and others, and can send approved reports to Vanta, ServiceNow and ComplyAssistant.

**Key features.**
- Rule checks before action, review routing with escalation of late reviews, and signed callbacks that return review decisions to your application.
- An Agent Gateway you run in your own network, MCP and A2A support, SDKs, and guardrail flags from tools such as Lakera and Guardrails AI.
- Shadow mode to test a rule on live activity before it blocks anything.
- OpenTelemetry export, Splunk business-record feeds and Langfuse trace attachment.
- An important honest line on its own page: "Your application still does the work." VeriProof returns a decision; your code must act on it, and approval and execution have separate records.

**Pricing (veriproof.app/pricing, as of November 27, 2026).** Production contracts are annual and invoiced.

| Plan | Price | Includes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Developer | Free for 60 days | Sample data, 1,000 recorded sessions a month, 14-day retention, unlimited users and agents, Agent Gateway |
| Production Control | $995 per month, billed annually | 5 agents, 50,000 sessions a month, $8 per extra 1,000, 1-year retention, SSO, SDKs, signed callbacks |
| Portfolio Control | $2,500 per month, billed annually | 20 agents, 250,000 sessions a month, $4 per extra 1,000, 3-year retention, legal hold, custom roles, auditor links |
| Private Enterprise | From $90,000 per year | Runs in your own Azure subscription; Azure Marketplace listing "coming soon" |

**Pros**
- The most complete compliance and evidence package in the set.
- Reviews arrive where reviewers already work (Teams, Slack, ServiceNow, email).
- A 60-day free trial with enterprise features, no payment details.

**Cons**
- Priced for enterprises: the cheapest production plan is $995 a month on an annual contract.
- Heavier to integrate than a hook; you wire the check and outcome reporting into your application.
- Not aimed at an individual developer who wants to approve `rm -rf` from a phone.

**Benefits for your use case.** If a risk or compliance team has to sign off before agents touch money or claims, VeriProof gives them a shared record and a review workflow instead of a pile of Slack messages.

**Skip it if** you are a small team or solo developer, or you need an open source tool.

{% figure src="https://veriproof.app/veriproof-social-share-default.png" alt="VeriProof official social share image" caption="VeriProof's official share image. Source: veriproof.app" /%}

## 5. Crios: a self-hosted agent platform with approvals built into the runtime

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/crios.png" name="Crios" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Crios (Crios Pte. Ltd., registered in Singapore per its site footer) is a self-hosted agent orchestration platform. Approvals are one part of a bigger package: the whole platform runs on your Kubernetes from one Helm chart, agents run in Kata Containers microVM sandboxes, credentials come from your Vault, and every approval, credential event and decision goes to an append-only audit log that can be delivered to your SIEM by webhook.

**Who it is for.** Organizations that want agents running inside their own perimeter, under their SSO and spend caps, with approval chains defined as part of the agent rather than as a prompt.

**Key features (all vendor-described).**
- Tool approvals set to never, always or conditional, with assignees, quorum and timeout, and the approver can rewrite the arguments before approving.
- Input and output guardrails on every turn; budget gates that park a run at a cost or iteration limit and ask for a decision.
- An approvals inbox on the web and via Telegram, with unanswered approvals aging into a dead-letter queue that you can redrive.
- Least-privilege connected accounts: credentials scoped to one repository, project or mailbox and revocable, with sensitive actions still needing an owner's approval.
- It exposes itself as an MCP server, so you can start and steer work from Claude Code, Cursor or Claude Desktop. The site claims more than 250 integrations.

**Pricing (as of November 27, 2026).** I could not find public prices. The `crios.ai/pricing` URL returned a 404 page when I checked. The homepage describes two options: Crios Cloud (a dedicated bare-metal cluster operated by Crios on a monthly subscription) and Crios Enterprise (self-hosted, hardened, with an offline-verifiable license); both are by contact at support@crios.ai. I found no free tier or trial.

**Pros**
- Approvals, spend caps, sandboxing and credential scoping in one runtime, which fits a least-privilege posture.
- Argument editing at approval time is a useful control few others advertise.
- Air-gap friendly, with no call-home license.

**Cons**
- No public pricing or trial, and no independent reviews or public repo that I could find.
- It is a platform you adopt, not a gate you bolt on to an existing agent.
- Everything above is vendor description; I did not run it.

**Benefits for your use case.** If you are standing up cloud coding agents in your own cluster and want approvals, egress limits and scoped credentials from one vendor, Crios is the only product here that bundles all of that.

**Skip it if** you already have an agent stack and only need an approval step, or you need transparent self-serve pricing.

{% figure src="https://crios.ai/og-card.png" alt="Crios official social card" caption="Crios's official site card. Source: crios.ai" /%}

## 6. HumanLayer: a multiplayer workspace for coding agents (not a pure approval gate)

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/humanlayer.png" name="HumanLayer" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** HumanLayer is the team behind the `12-factor-agents` guide, and its name is still what many people search for when they want humans in the loop of agents. Today humanlayer.dev describes "a multiplayer coding agent IDE and cloud": it runs coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi) on your machines and lets a team steer them from one place, with tasks, sessions, plan artifacts and code diffs synced between a desktop app, a web app and a daemon on each host. A post from Dex Horthy (@dexhorthy) dated October 4, 2026 says you can now use HumanLayer directly from the Pi agent or OpenCode.

{% x id="2106842513575416019" /%}

I am including it because readers will look for it, and the current product is about team collaboration and review of agent work. I did not find an approval-API product page on the current site, so treat HumanLayer here as "human oversight of coding agents as a team," not "an approval gate you call from your own agent." The older `humanlayer/12-factor-agents` repo (27,454 stars as of November 27, 2026, license reported as NOASSERTION by GitHub) is a set of design principles that includes contacting humans with tool calls as a first-class pattern; it is a guide, not a product.

**Who it is for.** Engineering teams that already run several Claude Code or Codex sessions and want shared plans, reviews and session history.

**Key features.**
- Shared tasks that group agent sessions, artifacts and worktrees.
- Bring-your-own-key for Claude Code, Codex and other subscriptions, so there is no per-token bill from HumanLayer (per its FAQ).
- Plugins that mirror Claude Code, OpenCode and Pi sessions into HumanLayer (from the repo README).
- SOC 2 Type II compliance claimed on the site, with Enterprise adding SSO/SAML, audit logs and on-prem or private VPC.

```bash
brew trust humanlayer/humanlayer
brew install humanlayer/humanlayer/humanlayer
```

**Pricing (humanlayer.dev, as of November 27, 2026).**

| Plan | Price | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Starter | Free | Up to 3 team members, up to 200 sessions a month |
| Pro | $100 per user per month | Unlimited tasks and sessions, multi-repo workspaces, BYOK, remote daemons |
| Enterprise | Custom | SSO/SAML, audit logs, volume pricing, on-prem and private VPC |

**Pros**
- Real free tier for small teams, and a repo that is Apache-2.0 licensed with about 13,000 stars (12,969 as of November 27, 2026, last pushed November 23, 2026).
- Strong fit for teams, with BYOK so your existing agent subscriptions keep working.
- Mature community and a long public track record compared with the other new names here.

**Cons**
- Not a drop-in approval gate for your own production agents, which is what most readers of this post need.
- The site contradicts the repo on licensing: the homepage FAQ says "Not yet" to "Is HumanLayer open source?" while the repo README says Apache-2.0 and calls the repo the full stack. The homepage footer still reads 2025, so the FAQ may be stale.
- $100 per user per month is the highest per-seat price in this roundup.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your agents are coding agents and your team needs to review plans and diffs together before work lands, this is the collaboration layer. Pair it with a per-call gate such as Pushary or withHuman if you also want tool-level approvals.

**Skip it if** you want a drop-in approval API for customer-facing agents.

## 7. Permit.io MCP Gateway: per-tool human approval for MCP tool calls

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/permit-io.png" name="Permit.io" size=40 /%}


**What it is.** Permit.io is an authorization vendor (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC and policy-as-code) that also ships an MCP Gateway for governing which MCP tools agents may call. Its docs describe human-in-the-loop (HITL) approvals that hold a sensitive tool call, such as deleting records or changing production data, until a human approves or rejects it. Calls that do not need approval run without a pause, and the MCP client simply waits for the response, so you do not change any client configuration.

**Who it is for.** Platform and security teams that want one place to decide which agents may call which MCP servers and tools, with a human gate on the dangerous ones. This is the closest match in the roundup to least-privilege access control for cloud coding agents, because approval sits on top of an identity-and-permission model rather than replacing it.

**Key features (from docs.permit.io).**
- A HITL switch on each tool, plus a server-level approval policy: require approval for all tools, or for tools at or above a trust level (None, Low and above, Medium and above, High only).
- A fixed check order: Permit checks and the trust ceiling run first (a denied call never reaches Approvals), then approval rules, then an optional "Guardian" that grades the call for intent and can ask a human when unsure.
- Asks for at most one approval per call; approvers decide on a card in the admin dashboard, with an approval history and notifications.
- A person-level switch, "Ask me before this agent changes or deletes anything," which routes write approvals to the person by email, with a recent sign-in check (15 minutes by default) and passkey confirmation where set up.
- Safe defaults: a 5 minute default timeout that always rejects (never approves) on expiry, extendable in the last 60 seconds up to one hour in total; the gateway cancels if the agent disconnects or access is revoked; at most 50 pending requests per host by default.

**Pricing (permit.io/pricing and docs, as of November 27, 2026).** The docs state that HITL approvals are available on Enterprise plans, with access by demo. The pricing page lists a Community plan, free forever, with limits such as 1,000 MAU, 20 tenants and 3 environments, and an Enterprise plan by contact. A Pro tier is referenced ("All Pro Features, plus") but its price did not appear in the page text I fetched, so I am not quoting one. The page also lists free access for open source projects and discounts for non-profits and young startups.

**Pros**
- Approval is built on a real authorization model with trust levels, not on string matching alone.
- Fail-safe timeout behavior documented in plain terms.
- No client changes: it works at the MCP gateway.

**Cons**
- HITL is gated behind Enterprise, so there is no self-serve path to try it.
- Scope is MCP tool calls; it will not gate a non-MCP shell command or an in-process function.
- Approvers use the Permit admin dashboard; I found no Slack or phone approval flow in the docs page I read.

**Benefits for your use case.** For an organization that already uses or wants a central policy engine, you get approval plus identity, trust levels and audit logs in one place.

**Skip it if** your agents are mostly local coding agents calling shell commands, or you need a free self-serve gate.

{% figure src="https://www.permit.io/opengraph-image?c7c5d9b2de25f712" alt="Permit.io official site image" caption="Permit.io's official site image. Source: permit.io" /%}

## 8. LangGraph interrupts: pause anywhere in the graph

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/langgraph-interrupts.png" name="LangGraph interrupts" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** LangGraph is LangChain's graph-based agent framework. Its `interrupt()` function pauses a graph at any node, surfaces a JSON-serializable payload to the caller, saves state through a checkpointer, and waits indefinitely until you resume with `Command(resume=...)`. The resume value becomes the return value of `interrupt()` inside the node. The repo `langchain-ai/langgraph` is MIT licensed with 45,512 stars as of November 27, 2026 (last pushed November 26, 2026).

**Who it is for.** Teams already building agents as LangGraph graphs who want approval, review-and-edit or input collection at precise points in the workflow, and who are happy to build the reviewer interface.

**How it works (from docs.langchain.com).** You need a checkpointer to persist state, a `thread_id` in the config so the graph can load the right checkpoint, and an `interrupt()` call where you want to pause. The docs stress that the `thread_id` is your persistent cursor: reuse it to resume, use a new value to start a fresh thread. The payload you pass appears on `stream.interrupts` with event streaming, or under `__interrupt__` with the default `invoke()` API.

```python
from langgraph.types import interrupt, Command

def approval_node(state):
    # Pause and ask for approval; the payload must be JSON-serializable
    approved = interrupt("Do you approve this action?")
    return {"approved": approved}

config = {"configurable": {"thread_id": "thread-1"}}
result = graph.invoke({"input": "data"}, config=config, version="v2")
    # result.interrupts holds the payload; later, resume with the human's answer:
graph.invoke(Command(resume=True), config=config, version="v2")
```

The snippet follows the pattern in the LangGraph docs; I did not run it.

**Pricing.** Free: the library is MIT licensed. LangSmith and LangGraph hosting are separate commercial products whose prices I did not check for this post.

**Pros**
- Fine-grained: you can pause before a specific tool, after a model draft, or mid-node.
- Durable: state is checkpointed, so an approval can arrive hours later.
- The largest ecosystem in this roundup, and several products above (withHuman, Pushary, HumanAuth) list LangGraph adapters.

**Cons**
- You build everything on the human side: who is asked, over which channel, with what timeout, and the audit trail.
- The graph waits indefinitely by default, so a missing human means a stuck run unless you add timeouts.
- Only protects agents built in LangGraph.

**Benefits for your use case.** Zero extra vendor and complete control. It is the best choice when approval is part of a business workflow you already model as a graph.

**Skip it if** your agents are not LangGraph agents, or you need Slack, phone and audit features out of the box.

{% youtube id="QXmy8olBU44" title="Pause and Resume AI Agents with LangGraph Human-in-the-Loop (GenAI Practice)" /%}

Third-party tutorial on LangGraph's human-in-the-loop pause and resume. Title and channel confirmed through YouTube oEmbed; I did not watch it end to end.

## 9. OpenAI Agents SDK approvals: `needs_approval` and resumable run state

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/openai-agents-sdk-approvals.png" name="OpenAI Agents SDK approvals" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The OpenAI Agents SDK has a documented human-in-the-loop flow: tools declare when they need approval, run results surface pending approvals as `interruptions`, and `RunState` lets you serialize a paused run and resume it after decisions. The repo `openai/openai-agents-python` is MIT licensed with 30,902 stars as of November 27, 2026 (last pushed that same day).

**Who it is for.** Teams building on the OpenAI Agents SDK, including agents that hand off to other agents or use agents as tools.

**How it works (from the SDK docs).** Set `needs_approval=True` on a tool, or give it an async function that decides per call. If the arguments are missing, malformed or not a JSON object, the callable is not invoked and the call requires manual approval, so the rule fails closed. When a tool needs approval, the run pauses, and `RunResult.interruptions` lists `ToolApprovalItem` entries with the agent name, tool name and arguments. You convert the result to a `RunState`, call `state.approve(...)` or `state.reject(...)`, then resume with `Runner.run(agent, state)`. Approvals raised after a handoff or inside a nested `Agent.as_tool()` surface on the outer run. Local MCP servers support `require_approval`, and hosted MCP tools support it through `HostedMCPTool`.

```python
from agents import Agent
from agents.decorators import tool

@tool(needs_approval=True)
async def cancel_order(order_id: int) -> str:
    return f"Cancelled order {order_id}"

agent = Agent(name="Support agent", tools=[cancel_order])
```

The decorator form above is taken from the SDK documentation; I did not run it.

**Pricing.** Free: the SDK is MIT licensed. Model usage is billed separately by whichever provider you use.

**Pros**
- Fail-closed argument handling and per-call decisions scoped to a call ID.
- Strong production guidance: the docs say to keep the full snapshot on the server, authenticate the reviewer, and never accept replacement tool calls or serialized state from the client.
- Honest about an important failure mode: approval permits a call to run but does not guarantee exactly-once execution, so use idempotency keys for payments.

**Cons**
- You build the reviewer UI, routing and audit log.
- Sticky `always_approve` decisions for local shell and file-edit tools match tool type and name, not the agent, so they can apply to another agent after a handoff. The docs advise per-call decisions when executors differ.
- Local shell and `apply_patch` approval is opt-in: `needs_approval` defaults to `False`.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your agents already run on the Agents SDK, this is the shortest path to a real pause, with documented guidance on how not to build an insecure approval endpoint.

**Skip it if** you are not on the Agents SDK, or you want a managed reviewer experience.

## 10. Claude Code permission hooks: the local gate for coding agents

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-human-in-the-loop-tools-for-ai-agents-2026/logos/claude-code-permission-hooks.png" name="Claude Code permission hooks" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Claude Code has a hooks system: shell commands, HTTP calls or other handlers that run at lifecycle events. A `PreToolUse` hook can return a `permissionDecision` of `allow`, `deny`, `ask` or `defer`, and can also rewrite the tool input with `updatedInput`. A separate `PermissionRequest` event fires when a tool call needs a permission decision. Hooks can match tools by name, such as `Bash` or `Edit|Write`, and MCP tools appear as regular tools you can match with patterns like `mcp__memory__.*`. The `anthropics/claude-code` repo showed 157,310 stars as of November 27, 2026; GitHub reports no license field for it.

**Who it is for.** Anyone running Claude Code who wants a deterministic, code-defined gate: block `rm -rf`, force an ask on `git push`, auto-allow read-only commands. Several products above (Pushary, withHuman) install their own hooks to do exactly this and forward the "ask" to a phone or Slack.

**Key facts from the hooks reference (code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks).**
- The hook receives JSON on stdin, including `tool_name` and `tool_input`, and returns a decision in a `hookSpecificOutput` object.
- `ask` shows the permission prompt to the user, with your reason in the prompt; `deny` blocks the call and the reason goes to Claude; `defer` ends the turn so the tool can be resumed later and requires non-interactive mode (`-p`).
- When several PreToolUse hooks disagree, precedence is `deny`, then `defer`, then `ask`, then `allow`. Deny and ask rules in settings still apply whatever a hook returns.
- Exit code 2 blocks the call; any other non-zero exit does not count as an allow, and even a JSON `allow` cannot override a blocking error.

```bash
#!/bin/bash
COMMAND=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command')
if echo "$COMMAND" | grep -q 'rm -rf'; then
  jq -n '{
    hookSpecificOutput: {
      hookEventName: "PreToolUse",
      permissionDecision: "deny",
      permissionDecisionReason: "Destructive command blocked by hook"
    }
  }'
else
  exit 0   # no decision; normal permission flow applies
fi
```

**Pricing.** Hooks are a feature of Claude Code; you pay for Claude Code itself. I did not check Claude Code's plan prices for this post.

**Pros**
- Deterministic and local: it runs your code, so the model cannot talk its way past it.
- Can modify input, not just allow or deny.
- A good building block: third-party phone and Slack tools plug in here.

**Cons**
- Covers Claude Code only (other agents have their own mechanisms).
- A hook on your laptop gives you no team routing, no phone, and no shared audit trail unless you build it.
- Hook scripts are code you must write, test and maintain; a buggy matcher is a silent hole.

**Benefits for your use case.** For least-privilege coding agents it is the cheapest enforcement point: deny the dangerous, ask on the borderline, allow the rest. Combine it with sandboxing for anything that must not happen.

**Skip it if** you run a mix of agents or need approvals from other humans.

