## Head-to-head: the closest pairs

### withHuman vs Pushary: which to pick

Both put a phone or chat in front of a risky call. **Pick withHuman** if you want an open protocol, a self-hostable Apache-2.0 core, a free hosted tier and approvals for production agents (refunds, payments) as much as for coding agents. **Pick Pushary** if your pain is coding agents waiting while you are away, and you want lock-screen approvals plus a phone call for $9.99 a month. Pushary is closed source and has no free plan beyond a 3-day trial; withHuman is younger in public terms (23 stars) but cheaper to try. If you only run Claude Code on a paid Claude plan, check whether Claude Code Remote Control already covers you; Pushary's own pricing page says it might.

### HumanAuth vs VeriProof: which to pick

Both are about proving control, not just pausing. **Pick HumanAuth** when you want a cryptographic receipt for a specific high-value action and your executing service can verify it; it is usage priced with 1,000 free requests a month. **Pick VeriProof** when a risk or compliance team needs rule management, review routing, framework mappings and sealed records across many agents, and an annual contract from $995 a month is acceptable. HumanAuth is a developer-first primitive; VeriProof is a governance program.

### LangGraph interrupts vs OpenAI Agents SDK approvals: which to pick

The choice is almost always made by your framework. LangGraph pauses at any point in a graph and waits indefinitely, which suits workflows with several review steps. The OpenAI Agents SDK attaches approval to tools with `needs_approval`, fails closed on malformed arguments and ships explicit guidance on server-side storage and idempotency. If you are free to choose, LangGraph is more flexible and the Agents SDK documents its security caveats more thoroughly.

