**Sources.** Vendor homepages and pricing pages (withhuman.ai, pushary.com, humanauth.ai, veriproof.app, crios.ai, humanlayer.dev, permit.io), vendor documentation (docs.permit.io, docs.langchain.com, openai.github.io, code.claude.com), the GitHub API for stars, licenses and push dates, and public X posts from vendor and founder accounts. All of it was fetched on November 27, 2026.

**What I did.** I read each page and doc listed above, checked that each URL returns HTTP 200, queried the GitHub API for the repositories named, confirmed that the npm and PyPI packages mentioned for Pushary and HumanAuth exist, and verified every YouTube and X embed through YouTube's oEmbed endpoint and X's syndication endpoint. I confirmed each product's official site loads and describes approval for agents.

**What I did not do.** I did not install or run any product, hook or SDK, did not create accounts, and did not test latency, reliability or security. Capability statements are vendor-described. No neutral benchmark of approval tools exists that I could find, so the post has none. Numbers such as Pushary's 214 decisions and 3 second median latency come from the founder's own workspace and are vendor-reported.

**Ranking.** The order of sections puts purpose-built products first and framework features last. It is not a score. I have no affiliate or sponsored relationship with any product here.

**Product set.** The record for this post named ten products. I verified that all ten (withHuman, Pushary, HumanAuth, VeriProof, Crios, HumanLayer, LangGraph interrupts, OpenAI Agents SDK approvals, Claude Code permission hooks, Permit.io) exist and describe human approval, with the caveat that HumanLayer's current product is team collaboration around coding agents rather than a standalone approval API. No swaps were made.

## FAQ

### What is human-in-the-loop approval for AI agents?

It is a control that holds an agent's tool call until a person approves it. A good implementation intercepts the call, applies rules, routes the request to the right person, defaults to deny on silence, resumes the agent with the decision, and records who decided what.

### What is the difference between a human approval gate and just asking the model to confirm?

A model asking "are you sure?" in chat is not a control: the model can proceed, and the confirmation is not tied to a person. An approval gate runs outside the model, in a hook, gateway or application code, and the tool call does not execute without a recorded human decision.

### Which tool is best for approving Claude Code from my phone?

Pushary is built for that and supports iPhone lock-screen approvals and a phone call if you miss the push, from $9.99 a month. Claude Code also has its own Remote Control feature included with paid Claude plans, which Pushary's own pricing page says is enough if Claude Code is your only agent. withHuman also supports Claude Code hooks and has iPhone and Android apps.

### Is there a free human-in-the-loop tool for AI agents?

Yes. withHuman has a free hosted tier for up to 5 members and an open source self-hosted edition. HumanAuth gives 1,000 approval requests a month free. LangGraph and the OpenAI Agents SDK are MIT-licensed libraries with built-in pause and resume. Claude Code hooks are part of Claude Code. VeriProof offers a 60-day trial, and Pushary offers a 3-day trial.

### How do human approval gates work in a LangGraph agent?

You call `interrupt()` inside a node with a JSON-serializable payload. LangGraph saves state with a checkpointer, returns the payload to the caller, and waits until you invoke the graph again with `Command(resume=...)` using the same `thread_id`. The resume value becomes the return value of `interrupt()`.

### How do approvals work in the OpenAI Agents SDK?

You mark a tool with `needs_approval=True` or a callable. When the model calls it, the run pauses and `interruptions` lists the pending calls. You turn the result into a `RunState`, call `approve` or `reject`, and resume with `Runner.run`. The docs advise keeping the snapshot on your server, authenticating the reviewer, and using idempotency keys for side effects.

### Can Claude Code hooks require approval for specific commands?

Yes. A `PreToolUse` hook can return `ask` to show the permission prompt, `deny` to block, `allow` to skip the prompt, or `defer` to end the turn so the tool can be resumed later in non-interactive mode. You match tools by name, for example `Bash` or `mcp__.*`. Deny and ask rules in your settings still apply whatever the hook returns.

### What is the best way to apply least-privilege access to cloud coding agents?

Layer controls. Give the agent narrowly scoped credentials, run it in a sandbox, deny known-destructive commands in a hook, and put a human gate on the actions that remain risky. Among the products here, Crios bundles scoped credentials, microVM sandboxes and approvals, and Permit.io puts approval on top of trust levels at the MCP gateway. Both are vendor-described and I did not test them.

### What happens if nobody answers an approval request?

It depends on the tool. Permit.io's gateway rejects after a 5 minute default timeout and never approves on timeout. Pushary lets each rule choose keep waiting, deny, escalate or allow, and its call feature can ring you. LangGraph waits indefinitely unless you add a timer. HumanAuth uses TTLs from 10 seconds to 1 hour. Always test the timeout path before relying on it.

### Do I need cryptographic receipts for agent approvals?

Not for every action. A rule plus a logged yes is enough for low-risk work. Receipts matter when someone else, such as an auditor, a counterparty or a downstream service, must be able to verify that a specific human approved specific parameters. HumanAuth makes that its core design; VeriProof provides sealed records for a governance program.

### Are these new products safe to adopt?

Treat them as early. withHuman's launch post on X is dated October 15, 2026 and its repo had 23 stars as of November 27, 2026. Pricing pages and features are changing, some features are labelled "coming soon," and I found no independent reviews or security audits for several of them. Start with one non-critical action, keep the executing service as the final enforcer, and keep the option to switch (withHuman's open protocol and HumanAuth's offline-verifiable receipts both aim at this).

### How is Permit.io different from an approval tool like withHuman?

Permit.io is an authorization vendor first: its MCP Gateway applies identity, trust levels and permission checks to MCP tool calls, with approval as one step, and human-in-the-loop is available on Enterprise plans. withHuman is purpose-built for approvals across any agent, with a free tier. If you already need centralized authorization, Permit.io fits. If you need a quick gate for one risky action, withHuman or a hook is lighter.

### Why is HumanLayer in this list if it no longer looks like an approval API?

Because people searching for human-in-the-loop for agents still land on it, and because its current product (shared coding-agent sessions, plans and reviews) is a form of human oversight. I flag clearly that it is not a drop-in approval gate for your own production agents.

